VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: W4
ATTEMPT: 1
---
Baseline (green, not the reason for FAIL):
- go build ./... : exit 0
- go vet ./...   : exit 0
- go test ./...  : all packages ok
- make css-verify: "tailwind.css is up to date"

Sign convention (checked, correct): metrics.go computes cumulativeDelta,
healthcareCumulativeDelta and combinedCumulativeDelta all as actual-minus-target
(>0 = over) — internal/services/metrics/metrics.go lines 234-235, 241, 250. No
sign flip found; verdict.go's classifyBucket/classifyVerdictSentence consume
these consistently. Boundary tests in verdict_test.go correctly pin >eps vs >=eps
(0.99/1.00/1.01 living-boundary cases, 10%-ratio amber/red boundary).

Byte-identical no-target path (checked, correct): `git diff master -- web/templates/components/dashboard-verdict-bar.html`
shows the entire new block is wrapped in `{{if $v.HasTarget}}`; the "no target"
render test explicitly asserts absence of "Living:", "Healthcare:", "You could
spend", "No headroom", "of room comes from".

REAL DEFECT FOUND — banner and the pre-existing Budget KPI card disagree about
the same number on the same page.

kpis.html embeds the verdict bar directly (`web/templates/components/kpis.html`
line 3: `{{template "dashboard-verdict-bar" .}}`), so both render together. The
new banner classifies each bucket with `onBudgetEps` (verdict.go line 9-11,
comment: "matches the Budget KPI card's threshold"): a delta must exceed
±$1.00 to count as over/under, otherwise "on target". But the KPI card's own
per-bucket Living/Health lines (kpis.html lines 150-158, unchanged by this
task — confirmed via `git diff master -- web/templates/components/kpis.html`,
which touches only the Monthly-Living-Expenses provenance tooltip) use a bare
`gt ... 0.0` / `lt ... 0.0` comparison with NO dead-band. The comment's claim
that onBudgetEps "matches the Budget KPI card's threshold" is true only for the
card's *total* Budget swing (kpis.html line 138/140, which does use ±1.0) — it
is false for the per-bucket Living/Health rows the new banner text is supposed
to agree with.

Reproduced with a fixture (BuildBudgetVerdict + renderer.RenderToString("kpis", ...)):
  HealthcareCumulativeDelta = 0.60 (just barely over), CumulativeDelta = -3000
  (well under), CombinedCumulativeDelta = -2999.40 (net under).

Rendered banner (green, healthy):
  "Healthcare: $0.60 on target" ... "You could spend up to $2,999.40 extra
  this period and stay on plan."

Rendered Budget KPI card, same page, same $0.60 number:
  >Health: <span class="num">$1,500.60</span> of <span class="num">$1,500.00</span>
  <span class="text-rose-500 ...">$0.60 over</span>

The same $0.60 healthcare overage is simultaneously "on target" (green, banner)
and "over" (rose/red, card) in the same view, and the banner's headroom
sentence ("spend up to $2,999.40 extra ... stay on plan") is generated by
treating that bucket as clean. This is exactly the class of issue the task
asked to rule out ("Consistency with the card ... confirm banner decomposition
== card figures"): it does not hold for any per-bucket delta in the open
interval $0 < |delta| <= $1.00. Magnitude is small in this fixture but the
inconsistency is structural, not a rounding artifact, and it reflects a false
premise baked into the code comment justifying the shared epsilon.

Other attack-surface probes performed, no defect found in isolation:
- living $0.50-over + healthcare $0.40-over (combined $0.90 over, both
  individually within eps): renders Health=green, Sentence=both,
  UnderRoom=0 — headline says "On budget" with no dollar figure, consistent
  with the pre-existing dead-band; not misleading on its own, but see above
  for why the underlying bucket classification does not survive contact with
  the adjacent card.
- exactly $1.00 combined delta: same dead-band result (green, both, room=0),
  consistent with the documented eps semantics.
- refund-heavy window (living cumulative delta = -50000): produces "You could
  spend up to $50,000.00 extra" — mathematically follows from the existing
  metrics field and is not a new misstatement introduced by this task (the
  claim only promises figures sourced from existing DashboardMetrics fields,
  which this is), but it is an inherited honesty risk worth the boss's
  attention if not already accepted.
- unconfigured-bucket forcing: confirmed correct via existing test
  ("only living configured: healthcare's degenerate raw delta is ignored")
  and reproduced independently.

Verdict: FAIL on the KPI-card consistency criterion the task explicitly asked
to verify. The banner's per-bucket "on target" language can contradict the
adjacent, pre-existing KPI card's "over" label for the same dollar figure,
which is capable of misleading the user about whether they have headroom.
